<sect>crdtrans (Revision 1.1 -- 22/04/1998)


<p> The unit <tt/crdtrans/ regroups additional transformation routines
not included in <tt/trafo/. The units of all parameters used are
either meters or radians.

<sect1> Routines
<p>

<sect2>ellipse_circumference
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
double ellipse_circumference (const double a, const double ex)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>The function returns the circumference of an ellipsoid given the major axis <tt/a/ and
the excentricity <tt/ex/.

<sect2>center_angle
<p> 
<tscreen><verb>
double center_angle( const double lat1,
                     const double lon1,
                     const double lat2,
                     const double lon2)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
This function calculates the center angle in [radian] between
two points given by their sperical coordinates <tt/lat1, lon1/ 
resp. <tt/lat2, lon2/ based on spherical cosine.

<sect2>ellipse_approxdist
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
double ellipse_approxdist (const double lat1,const double lon1,
			   const double lat2,const double lon2,
			   const struct ellipsoid ell)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
This functions returns the ellipsoidal distance between 2 points on
the ellipsoid. (approximated value based on spherical approximation
using the curvature at the mean latitude value of 2 points). Input
parameters are the latitude <tt/lat1/, longitude <tt/lon1/ of 
the first point and latitude <tt/lat2/, longitude <tt/lon2/ of
the second point. <tt/ell/ contains the ellipsoid definition.

<sect2>xyz_to_azel
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int xyz_to_azel (double xyz1[3], double xyz2[3],  struct ellipsoid ell,
		 double *dist, double *elev, double *azi)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Given 2 points in geocentrical coordinates <tt/xyz1/ and <tt/xyz2/
and ellipsoidal parameters <tt/ell/, the distance <tt/dist/, the
elevation <tt/ele/, and the azimuth <tt/azi/ form point 1 to point 2
are calculated with respect to the ellipsoidal normal direction. 
The elevation angle is <tt/0/ at the horizon and <tt>PI/2</tt> 
at he zenith. The azimuth angle is <tt/0/ in North direction 
and clockwise incrementing.

<sect2>azel_to_xyz
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int azel_to_xyz (double xyz[3], double azi, double ele,
		 double dxyz[3], stuct ellipsoid ell)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>


<sect2>top_to_xyz
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int top_to_xyz (double lat, double lon,	double dtop[3],	double dxyz[3])
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Conversion of topocentric coordinates <tt/dtop/ of a vector 
to its corresponding geocentric coordinates. The orientation
of the topocentric vector : dtop[0] =<tt/East/ / <tt/dtop[1]/ =  North 
/ <tt/dtop[2]/= up. Latitude <tt/lat/ and longitude <tt/lon/ define
the orientation of the topocentric z-axis with respect to
the geocentrical frame. 

<sect2>xyz_to_top<em/x/
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int xyz_to_top (double lat, double lon, double dxyz[3], double dtop[3])
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Conversion of cartesion coordinates <tt/dxyz/ of a vector to its 
corresponding geocentric coordinates. The orientation
of the topocentric vector : dtop[0] =<tt/East/ / <tt/dtop[1]/ =  North 
/ <tt/dtop[2]/= up. Latitude <tt/lat/ and longitude <tt/lon/ define
the orientation of the topocentric z-axis with respect to
the geocentrical frame. 

<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int xyz_to_top2 (double xyz[3], double dxyz[3], double dtop[3], struct ellipsoid ell)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Conversion of cartesion coordinates <tt/dxyz/ of a vector to its 
corresponding geocentric coordinates. The orientation
of the topocentric vector : dtop[0] =<tt/East/ / <tt/dtop[1]/ =  North 
/ <tt/dtop[2]/= up. Instead of latitude and longitude as 
in function <tt/xyz_to_top/ the geocentric coordinates <tt/xyz/ and
a ellipsoid <tt/ell/ are passed to the routine. (They are converted
locally to latitude and longitude).

